\title{Death Avoidance as the Dominant Routing Signal:\\A Counterfactual Analysis on ScienceWorld}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\input{abstract}
\input{introduction}
\input{method}
\input{experiments}
\input{related_work}
\input{discussion}
\input{conclusion}

\section*{Limitations}

Our analysis uses a single benchmark (ScienceWorld) with a single LLM backbone (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct), and generalization to other interactive environments and model scales remains to be established. A preliminary cross-model pilot with Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct ($N{=}10$ episode pairs, 2 task types) shows directionally consistent death patterns (\S\ref{sec:cross-model}), but full-scale multi-model validation remains future work. The $-100$ death penalty may amplify DAF; environments with less catastrophic penalties could exhibit lower DAF, though the qualitative finding may still hold. On the 135 episodes (55.1\%) where neither strategy leads to catastrophic failure, deterministic computation retains a small advantage ($\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +2.84$), indicating non-zero routing signal beyond death avoidance. Our analysis covers 11 of 30 ScienceWorld task types; the remaining 19 (63\%) were excluded during prescreening because both strategies timed out on all seed variations within the 30-step budget, providing no counterfactual signal. These tasks likely require longer-horizon planning; increasing the step budget could surface additional routing signal whose composition may differ from the death-dominated pattern observed here. Stronger models (e.g., 70B-scale or reasoning-specialized) likely exhibit lower internal-strategy death rates, which would reduce DAF. Our finding may reflect a capability-dependent failure mode: at higher capability levels, death avoidance may no longer dominate routing signal.

\paragraph{Cross-environment scope.}
Our MATH and ALFWorld analyses (\S\ref{sec:discussion}) provide cross-environment evidence but do not conclusively establish generalization. In MATH (no death penalty), the deterministic advantage vanishes with a marginally significant reversal favoring internal reasoning ($+4.3$\,pp, $p = 0.079$, not reaching $\alpha{=}0.05$). In ALFWorld, the deterministic advantage stems from a capability gap rather than death avoidance. Environments with graded failure costs or intermediate penalty regimes remain unstudied.

\paragraph{Reasoning strategy scope.}
Our internal-reasoning baseline uses single-pass ReAct \citep{react-2023}, which lacks self-correction or backtracking. More sophisticated reasoning strategies---such as Reflexion-style self-correction \citep{reflexion-2023} or Tree-of-Thought backtracking \citep{tot-2023}---may reduce the internal strategy's death rate, which would lower DAF. Our findings may therefore be partly specific to the vulnerability of single-pass reasoning rather than a universal property of internal computation.

\paragraph{Step-level mislabeling.} Episode-level labels may assign suboptimal routing decisions to individual steps; in particular, steps preceding a death event are labeled det-preferred even if the death was not foreseeable from those steps' observations. Training on pure-strategy trajectories but deploying on mixed policy also introduces a distribution shift \citep{dagger-2011}.

\paragraph{Scope.}
Extending the counterfactual methodology to environments with graded failure costs, embodied agents, or multi-turn dialogue systems would clarify how broadly death avoidance accounts for apparent routing signal. Step-level labeling with denser rewards and the logit--generation gap in the implicit router are natural next steps. We audit labeling quality in Appendix~\ref{app:labeling}.

\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work uses ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, a publicly available text-based simulation environment, and does not involve human subjects or private data. All language models are accessed via standard APIs or publicly released weights. We report computational costs: approximately 40 GPU-hours on two consumer GPUs for data collection, router training, and evaluation. Code and data will be released upon acceptance.

\bibliography{references}

\appendix

\section{Labeling Quality Audit}
\label{app:labeling}

A potential concern is oracle circularity: whether episode-level labels reflect tie-breaking artifacts rather than genuine modality differences. We audit all 245 labels. Of the 150 \texttt{no\_preference} labels (61.2\%), 115 (46.9\%) are both-alive ties and 35 (14.3\%) are both-dead---all excluded from router training. The remaining 95 training labels are entirely genuine: 75 from death asymmetry (70 deterministic-preferred, 5 internal-preferred) and 20 from score comparison with both strategies surviving (16 deterministic-preferred, 4 internal-preferred). Zero labels depend on tie-breaking. Retraining the router using only death-asymmetry labels yields balanced accuracy of 57.3\%, within 1$\sigma$ of the full-label retrain (57.6 $\pm$ 0.5\%), confirming that score-comparison labels contribute negligible additional signal. The 97.4\% step-level tie rate (\S\ref{sec:collection}) further motivates episode-level over step-level labeling.

\section{Per-Task Decomposition}
\label{app:per-task}
We select 11 of ScienceWorld's 30 task types via prescreening: task types where both strategies complete at least one episode within the 30-step budget are retained; types where both strategies time out on all seed variations are excluded.\footnote{Prescreening ensures that the counterfactual comparison is meaningful---task types where neither strategy makes progress provide no routing signal.}

