\begin{abstract}
Modular LLM agents that route between neural reasoning and deterministic computation promise improved reliability, yet understanding \emph{what} drives the value of routing decisions remains unclear. We propose episode-level counterfactual analysis: running complete episodes under pure strategies and comparing final outcomes to decompose routing signal into death-avoidance and non-death components. Within completable-horizon tasks (11 of 30 ScienceWorld task types, 245 episodes, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct), roughly four-fifths of non-tie routing labels arise from death asymmetry alone---a penalty-independent measure. The penalty-sensitive Death Avoidance Factor reaches ${\sim}90\%$ at the default penalty $P{=}100$, with per-task correlation $r = 0.97$ ($p < 10^{-6}$) between death-rate differential and performance gap; we note that this correlation is partly structural, as death penalties contribute directly to score differences (\S\ref{sec:death-decomp}). A trained router achieves only 57--59\% balanced accuracy, consistent with extreme class imbalance induced by death dominance. Cross-environment analysis on MATH ($N{=}300$) shows routing signal vanishes without a death penalty: the label distribution inverts from 86:9 (deterministic-preferred) to 30:17 (internal directionally favored), with a marginally significant reversal of $+4.3$ percentage points ($p = 0.079$). A Llama-3.1-8B pilot provides preliminary directional evidence that death-driven routing generalizes across model families. These findings indicate that in environments with catastrophic failure modes, routing value is dominated by safety---the router functions primarily as a safety critic rather than an efficiency optimizer.
\end{abstract}
